.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt GZONED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gzoned
.Nd "emulate a host-managed zoned device on top of a regular provider"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm create
.Op Fl uv
.Op Fl s Ar zonesize
.Op Fl c Ar zones
.Op Fl m Ar maxopen
.Ar dev
.Nm
.Cm fault
.Op Fl v
.Fl z Ar zone
.Fl s Ar state
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm destroy
.Op Fl fv
.Ar name ...
.Nm
.Cm stop
.Op Fl fv
.Ar name ...
.Nm
.Cm clear
.Op Fl v
.Ar dev ...
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm load
.Nm
.Cm unload
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility presents to the system a host-managed zoned block device backed by a
regular, non-zoned provider.
The exposed device is of the kind described by the
SCSI Zoned Block Commands (ZBC) and ATA Zoned ATA Command Set (ZAC)
specifications.
.Pp
The backing provider is divided into equally sized, sequential-write-required
zones, unless marked conventional by the
.Fl c
option.
For each zone the GEOM class maintains a write pointer and answers the
zone-management commands
.Pq see Xr zonectl 8
issued through the
.Dv DIOCZONECMD
ioctl.
.Pp
Resetting a write pointer issues a best-effort
.Dv BIO_DELETE
for the zone's range to the backing provider, resembling a real drives
de-allocating a reset zone.
.Dv BIO_DELETE
requests to the zoned device itself are rejected, as zoned devices have no
unmap.
Sequential zones are reclaimed by resetting the write pointer.
.Pp
The configuration for a device is persistent.
A metadata block and the per-zone state are stored in a small area reserved at
the tail of the backing provider, so the zoned device is recreated
automatically whenever the provider appears
.Pq at boot, module load or hot-plug ,
with its write pointers restored.
Note that because of the metadata location on the backing provider, the zoned
device is torn down if the backing provider is resized with unflushed zone state
changes being discarded.
Zone state changes are committed to the backing provider on a BIO_FLUSH.
Changes made since the last flush may be rolled back by an unclean shutdown,
resembling a drive whose zone state is volatile until flushed.
.Pp
The first argument to
.Nm
indicates an action to be performed:
.Bl -tag -width ".Cm destroy"
.It Cm create
Mark
.Ar dev
as zoned, creating a device named after the backing provider with
.Dq .zoned
appended
.Pq e.g. Pa /dev/da0.zoned .
The number of zones is the usable size of
.Ar dev
divided by
.Ar zonesize ;
a small reservation at the tail of
.Ar dev
holds the zone metadata; any trailing space that does not make up a full zone
is not exposed.
Host-managed zoned providers are refused: their tail normally lies in a
sequential-write-required zone and cannot hold the metadata and zone state
table.
.It Cm fault
Inject a zone fault into the running device
.Ar name .
.Fl z Ar zone
selects the zone by number and
.Fl s Ar state
is one of
.Ar ro
.Pq Ar readonly ,
.Ar offline ,
.Ar reset
.Pq zone needs a write-pointer reset
or
.Ar clear
.Pq return the zone to its normal state implied by its write pointer .
Readonly and offline zones reject writes and zone-management commands;
the fault persists with the rest of the zone state until cleared.
.It Cm clear
Permanently un-zone the given backing device(s) by clearing the on-disk
metadata.
A backing device carrying a live zoned device is held open and cannot be
cleared: tear the device down with
.Cm stop
first.
.It Cm stop
Tear down the given zoned device(s) without touching the on-disk metadata.
.Ar name
is the zoned device
.Pq e.g. Pa da0.zoned .
Unflushed zone state is committed to the backing provider first.
The device comes back the next time the backing provider is tasted
.Pq e.g. at boot or when it is opened and closed again .
.It Cm destroy
Same as
.Cm stop .
.It Cm list
See
.Xr geom 8 .
.It Cm status
See
.Xr geom 8 .
.It Cm load
See
.Xr geom 8 .
.It Cm unload
See
.Xr geom 8 .
.El
.Pp
Additional options:
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl s Ar zonesize"
.It Fl c Ar zones
Mark the given zones as conventional
.Pq randomly writable, no write pointer .
.Ar zones
is a comma-separated list of zone numbers and inclusive ranges, counted
from zero, e.g.\&
.Ar 60 ,
.Ar 60-70
or
.Ar 0-3,60,90-95 .
At most 16 comma-separated entries may be given.
By default all zones are sequential-write-required.
.It Fl f
Tear down the device even if it is still open.
.It Fl m Ar maxopen
Limit the number of simultaneously open sequential zones.
Once the limit is reached, implicitly opened zones are implicitly closed to make
room.
If every open zone was opened explicitly, further writes and explicit opens fail
with
.Er ENOSPC .
The default of 0 means unlimited.
.It Fl s Ar zonesize
Size of a single zone.
The value must be a multiple of the backing provider's sector size and may use
the usual size suffixes
.Pq e.g. Ar 7G , 64M .
The default is
.Ar 256M .
.It Fl u
Restrict reads in sequential-write-required zones
.Pq unset URSWRZ bit .
Similarly to real zoned drives, the device supports unrestricted reads that are
passed through unchecked by default.
With
.Fl u
the device instead reports URSWRZ as unset and rejects with
.Er EIO
any read in a sequential zone that extends beyond that zone's write pointer, as
well as any read that crosses out of a conventional zone into a sequential one.
Note that this renders the device unreadable to zone-unaware consumers.
Tasting the zoned provider
.Pq e.g. partition table scanning
will also fail until the accessed zones have been written.
.It Fl v
Be more verbose.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following
.Xr sysctl 8
variable can be used to control the behavior of the
.Nm ZONED
GEOM class.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va kern.geom.zoned.debug : No 0
Debug level of the
.Nm ZONED
GEOM class.
This can be set to a number between 0 and 3 inclusive.
If set to 0, minimal debug information is printed; if set to 3, the maximum
amount of debug information is printed.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Expose the memory disk
.Pa /dev/md0
as a host-managed zoned device
.Pa /dev/md0.zoned
with one-gigabyte zones, the first four of which are conventional.
Inspect its zones, then permanently un-zone it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mdconfig -t malloc -s 10G -u md0
gzoned create -s 1G -c 0-3 /dev/md0
zonectl -d /dev/md0.zoned -c params
zonectl -d /dev/md0.zoned -c rz
gzoned clear /dev/md0
.Ed
.Pp
The same operations are also available through the
.Xr geom 8
front end:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
geom zoned create -s 128M /dev/da0
geom zoned stop da0.zoned
geom zoned clear /dev/da0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr geom 4 ,
.Xr md 4 ,
.Xr geom 8 ,
.Xr mdconfig 8 ,
.Xr zonectl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility appeared in
